      {
        "heading": "5 DISCUSSION",
        "text": "Here, we introduced a novel CNN architecture that uses a biophysical photoreceptor model at its front end to leverage photoreceptor adaptation mechanisms and enable generalization across light levels. We found that this model strongly outperforms other CNN architectures, including ones that make heavy use of normalization layers, in generalizing across light levels.\nOur photoreceptor-CNN retina model is able to function well at light levels very different from those at which it was trained, making it a potentially desirable tool for many applications, including visual prosthetics. Those could benefit substantially from algorithms that can translate visual stimuli into patterns of nerve impulses (to be stimulated by the device in order to restore sight), and that work well under varied lighting conditions.\nMoreover, the photoreceptor-CNN model can provide the scientific community with an opportunity to investigate visual processing of dynamic stimuli. For example, how much of the adaptation in the retina can be accounted for by photoreceptors alone? and how much does the sensitivity of each RGC to different stimulus features vary as the lighting conditions change? From a wider neuroscience perspective, our approach demonstrates the power of embedding neural dynamics in deep learning models of the brain: our new biophysical model layer can match its sensitivity to prevailing input conditions that change dynamically whereas the downstream layers of the model progressively extract relevant features from those dynamically adapting input stages.\nOur model also potentially enables better computer vision systems for applications where lighting is highly dynamic, such as the object detection and recognition systems on autonomous vehicles. Here, pixel-wise local luminance adaptation (from using our photoreceptor model as an input layer) could help filter out disruptive lighting changes induced by changing shadows or clouds, that would otherwise cause errors in object recognition and/or detection (Janai et al., 2020; Gomez-Ojeda et al., 2015; Kolaman et al., 2019). A proof-of-concept demonstration that a photoreceptor-inspired convolution layer, Adaptive-Conv, may be better than conventional CNN layers at filtering out lighting fluctuations from a visual scene, is presented in Appendix C.1.\nCODE AVAILABILITY\nThe photoreceptor-CNN model and Adaptive-Conv model can be downloaded from [Redacted for Double-Blind Review].\nETHICS STATEMENT\nAll electrophysiology experiments were performed in accordance with the guidelines of Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee at [Redacted for Double-Blind Review]."
